<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Plutarch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 46–after 119</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Lucius Mestrius Plutarchus, Plutarch of Chaeronea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Plutarch was a Greek writer and philosopher from Chaeronea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, who also served as a priest at Delphi. His Parallel Lives pair biographies of famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and his Moralia gather essays on ethics, religion and customs. His works are a major source for ancient history and strongly shaped later European ideas of the classical world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plutarch.jpg" alt="Plutarch" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zde · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_philosopher,_maybe_Plutarch,_2nd_century_BC,_AM_Delphi,_0135.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its chief officer was the general (strategos), elected annually, who commanded the army and presided over the assemblies; he could not hold office in consecutive years, so leaders such as Aratus, general seventeen times by <b>Plutarch</b>'s count, served in alternate years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Zeno of Citium, whose pupil Persaeus lived at his court; the poet Aratus of Soli wrote his astronomical poem, the Phaenomena, there; and the historian Hieronymus of Cardia, who served three generations of the dynasty, wrote the history of the Successors on which Diodorus and <b>Plutarch</b> later drew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Boeotia also gave Greek literature Hesiod, Pindar and, under Roman rule, <b>Plutarch of Chaeronea</b>, whose lives of Pelopidas and Epaminondas, the latter now lost, shaped the later image of the Theban heroes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Religion centred on the Baalat Gebal and on male gods later identified with Adonis and with Osiris; according to <b>Plutarch</b>, the chest containing the body of Osiris washed ashore at Byblos.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Apollo had two priests appointed for life, one of them later <b>Plutarch</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delphi">Delphi · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Menander's rule was remembered as benevolent: the Milinda Panha has him debating the monk Nagasena, converting and attaining arhatship, and <b>Plutarch</b> says that when he died the cities under his rule contested the honour of his remains and divided his ashes among their monuments, as with the Buddha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chaeronea, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.5,22.84&amp;year=93" aria-label="Chaeronea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5118 782 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Plutarch"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5568 1052h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5568 1052h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.5,22.84&amp;year=93">What was here in 93 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It must be borne in mind that my design is not to write histories, but lives. And the most glorious exploits do not always furnish us with the clearest discoveries of virtue or vice in men…&#8221;</p><footer>Life of Alexander &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Reputation is like flame. There is no difficulty in keeping it alive; it merely requires a little feeding with fuel. But let either of them become extinct and cold, and it will take some trouble to rekindle.&#8221;</p><footer>On Old Men in Public Life (Moralia) &#183; tr. T. G. Tucker, 1913 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/62618/pg62618-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For it does not necessarily follow, that, if a piece of work please for its gracefulness, therefore he that wrought it deserves our admiration.&#8221;</p><footer>Life of Pericles &#183; tr. John Dryden and others, rev. A. H. Clough, 1859 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/674/pg674-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
